1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:06,000
We transform it into sugar, fuel, plastic,

2
00:00:06,001 --> 00:00:10,000
and enough foodstuff to fill a supermarket.

3
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:11,000
It doesn't exist naturally.

4
00:00:11,001 --> 00:00:14,000
Corn is entirely a man-made phenomenon.

5
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:20,000
We grow it on the farm, in a lab, or even underground,

6
00:00:20,001 --> 00:00:26,000
on the cob, in a can, in a sack, or in a silo.

7
00:00:26,001 --> 00:00:28,000
It's a worldwide gold rush.

8
00:00:28,001 --> 00:00:34,000
Ethanol is about ready to take over as the number one user of corn in the United States.

9
00:00:34,001 --> 00:00:38,000
Now, corn on Modern Marvels.

10
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:52,000
Corn is the largest agricultural crop in the United States.

11
00:00:52,001 --> 00:00:57,300
fields of corn cover more than 80 million acres of land.

12
00:00:57,400 --> 00:01:01,740
That's an area larger than the state of New Mexico.

13
00:01:01,840 --> 00:01:04,120
Pass that corn on the cob.

14
00:01:04,220 --> 00:01:06,620
It's the delicious treat we call sweet corn.

15
00:01:08,680 --> 00:01:11,960
Lakeside Foods in Wisconsin has been processing sweet corn

16
00:01:12,060 --> 00:01:14,660
for over 100 years.

17
00:01:14,760 --> 00:01:18,560
Here, corn is king.

18
00:01:18,561 --> 00:01:21,860
This is where corn comes to get canned.

19
00:01:21,960 --> 00:01:24,400
And to make sure your sweet corn stays sweet,

20
00:01:24,500 --> 00:01:27,300
it's an all-out war against the clock,

21
00:01:27,400 --> 00:01:29,900
starting with the instant it's harvested.

22
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:31,900
The critical time for processing

23
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:33,300
is within five or six hours.

24
00:01:33,400 --> 00:01:35,740
We want to retain the sweetness of the corn.

25
00:01:35,840 --> 00:01:38,220
We don't want the sugars to turn to starch

26
00:01:38,320 --> 00:01:40,620
by being delayed in harvest.

27
00:01:40,720 --> 00:01:42,160
This is the first part of the process,

28
00:01:42,260 --> 00:01:43,920
as we've had the corn wait.

29
00:01:44,020 --> 00:01:45,580
We're loading it on the dock.

30
00:01:45,680 --> 00:01:47,720
Now we're starting to push it into a conveyor.

31
00:01:47,721 --> 00:01:51,120
a conveyor, a vibrating conveyor.

32
00:01:51,220 --> 00:01:52,820
This vibrating conveyor system

33
00:01:52,920 --> 00:01:55,520
causes the cobs to continually rotate,

34
00:01:55,620 --> 00:01:57,860
so the weight of the cob doesn't damage the kernels

35
00:01:57,960 --> 00:01:58,960
resting on the belt.

36
00:02:02,060 --> 00:02:05,240
Once inside the processing plant, the sweet ears of corn

37
00:02:05,340 --> 00:02:08,580
journey into a carnival of precision automation.

38
00:02:08,680 --> 00:02:10,280
As the corn comes into the corn room,

39
00:02:10,380 --> 00:02:11,880
we're going into the Husker.

40
00:02:11,980 --> 00:02:15,520
The Husker has six lanes, as the lanes are separated

41
00:02:15,521 --> 00:02:18,460
by a series of neoprene and rubber rolls.

42
00:02:18,560 --> 00:02:20,660
What the rolls are doing in opposite directions

43
00:02:20,760 --> 00:02:23,360
are stripping the silk and the husk material

44
00:02:23,460 --> 00:02:25,160
off the air of corn.

45
00:02:25,260 --> 00:02:28,460
Then it goes into a conveyor to further process.

46
00:02:28,560 --> 00:02:30,360
On average, in an hour, we're processing

47
00:02:30,460 --> 00:02:34,980
approximately 35 tons of corn out of the four huskers.

48
00:02:35,080 --> 00:02:37,580
Surprisingly, almost all of the delicate corn kernels

49
00:02:37,680 --> 00:02:40,080
remain intact after this seemingly violent step.

50
00:02:42,820 --> 00:02:45,520
Next stop, the cutter.

51
00:02:45,521 --> 00:02:47,620
Ears must be positioned in the correct direction

52
00:02:47,621 --> 00:02:51,760
to meet the fast and furious razor-sharp blades.

53
00:02:51,761 --> 00:02:53,820
Each cutter has a separate cutting head,

54
00:02:53,821 --> 00:02:55,760
which includes six blades.

55
00:02:55,761 --> 00:02:58,760
These blades are moved by a head in a gear system,

56
00:02:58,761 --> 00:03:00,460
which will open and close depending

57
00:03:00,461 --> 00:03:02,580
on the taper of the year.

58
00:03:02,581 --> 00:03:06,400
Like magic, the ear goes in, and out

59
00:03:06,401 --> 00:03:09,380
come perfect corn kernels, still in a race

60
00:03:09,381 --> 00:03:13,220
to their ultimate destination.

61
00:03:13,221 --> 00:03:15,040
The cob will join the husks and silks

62
00:03:15,041 --> 00:03:18,140
on their own conveyor, bound to become part of a smorgasbord

63
00:03:18,240 --> 00:03:18,940
for livestock.

64
00:03:21,740 --> 00:03:24,280
As we convey the kernels over to the next piece of equipment,

65
00:03:24,380 --> 00:03:27,180
it is a shaker, which actually is separating kernels

66
00:03:27,280 --> 00:03:30,480
and is removing pieces of top, tank, plus material that may

67
00:03:30,580 --> 00:03:33,500
still be in the product's screen.

68
00:03:33,600 --> 00:03:36,460
Next, a series of wet washers and air cleaners

69
00:03:36,560 --> 00:03:38,300
propel the corn into the inspection area.

70
00:03:41,300 --> 00:03:44,640
Cameras reject bad kernels as they fly by at 600 feet per

71
00:03:44,641 --> 00:03:49,940
minute, and human inspectors perform a final screening.

72
00:03:50,040 --> 00:03:52,180
After the kernels leave inspection,

73
00:03:52,280 --> 00:03:56,020
they're submerged in a flume of hot water that kills bacteria

74
00:03:56,120 --> 00:03:58,820
and raises the corn's temperature in anticipation

75
00:03:58,920 --> 00:03:59,860
of the cooking process.

76
00:04:03,220 --> 00:04:05,820
Now the sweet kernels are ready for the can.

77
00:04:05,920 --> 00:04:08,200
The critical quality control program

78
00:04:08,300 --> 00:04:11,740
is to maintain a certain head space in the can.

79
00:04:11,840 --> 00:04:14,140
We need a temperature of a minimum of 80 degrees.

80
00:04:14,141 --> 00:04:15,940
before this goes to the cooker.

81
00:04:16,040 --> 00:04:19,280
We also need a maximum fill weight of 10.7 ounces.

82
00:04:19,380 --> 00:04:22,720
So those are critical factors in the operation of filling

83
00:04:22,820 --> 00:04:25,960
and seaming this can.

84
00:04:26,060 --> 00:04:27,480
In the cooker, the kernels bounce

85
00:04:27,580 --> 00:04:34,460
inside each spiraling can in 225 degree heat for 16 minutes.

86
00:04:34,560 --> 00:04:39,140
380 cans per minute enter the cooker.

87
00:04:39,240 --> 00:04:40,060
The result?

88
00:04:40,160 --> 00:04:42,700
Perfect corn in hermetically sealed cans,

89
00:04:42,701 --> 00:04:44,500
with a shelf life of at least three years.

90
00:04:47,800 --> 00:04:50,000
Total time elapsed from its arrival at Lakeside?

91
00:04:52,840 --> 00:04:55,940
25 minutes.

92
00:04:56,040 --> 00:04:58,740
This Lakeside production plant will process approximately

93
00:04:58,840 --> 00:05:03,780
40,000 tons of corn in 60 days or less.

94
00:05:03,880 --> 00:05:07,160
Each year in the United States, almost 3 million tons of sweet

95
00:05:07,260 --> 00:05:10,860
corn are canned, frozen, and packaged.

96
00:05:10,861 --> 00:05:13,600
That is a lot of corn.

97
00:05:13,700 --> 00:05:15,360
But here's the surprise.

98
00:05:15,460 --> 00:05:19,060
It represents less than 1% of all commercial corn grown

99
00:05:19,160 --> 00:05:20,600
in the United States.

100
00:05:20,700 --> 00:05:23,900
So what's growing in all those corn fields?

101
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:28,940
It's called dent corn, so named for a physical dimple

102
00:05:29,040 --> 00:05:32,620
on the end of the kernel.

103
00:05:32,720 --> 00:05:35,020
Sweet corn is best when it's full of moisture

104
00:05:35,120 --> 00:05:37,820
and eaten right off the cob.

105
00:05:37,821 --> 00:05:42,820
But Dent, commonly known as field corn, is just the opposite.

106
00:05:45,800 --> 00:05:51,260
The US grows almost 364 million tons of field corn per year.

107
00:05:51,360 --> 00:05:54,400
That's about 110 pounds for every person on the planet.

108
00:05:57,940 --> 00:06:01,040
Field corn is an ingredient in over 3,000 foodstuffs

109
00:06:01,140 --> 00:06:04,020
on the grocery store shelf.

110
00:06:04,120 --> 00:06:06,980
You'll find it in everything from breakfast cereals

111
00:06:06,981 --> 00:06:12,280
to soda, to the batter on fish sticks, and even yogurt

112
00:06:12,380 --> 00:06:15,280
and ice cream.

113
00:06:15,380 --> 00:06:19,320
Every box of corn flakes contains 97% corn,

114
00:06:19,420 --> 00:06:21,220
and the cornmeal surrounding a corn dog

115
00:06:21,320 --> 00:06:24,240
is a fine grind of dent kernels.

116
00:06:24,340 --> 00:06:27,900
Corn starch sticks energy bars together,

117
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:31,240
and corn oil will fry your chicken.

118
00:06:31,340 --> 00:06:33,940
It's all field corn.

119
00:06:33,941 --> 00:06:38,080
And more than 15% of America's field corn is shipped around the globe.

120
00:06:39,420 --> 00:06:41,920
Corn is the most important crop in the world.

121
00:06:42,420 --> 00:06:46,260
Farmers produce more corn than any other crop by a pretty long shot.

122
00:06:46,420 --> 00:06:49,460
Wheat comes in kind of second, but kind of distant to corn.

123
00:06:49,660 --> 00:06:53,560
And the United States produces 40% of the world's corn.

124
00:06:55,020 --> 00:07:00,860
In fact, a single U.S. corn farmer provides enough food and fiber for 143 people.

125
00:07:00,861 --> 00:07:04,860
But as much corn as humans eat,

126
00:07:04,960 --> 00:07:08,460
it's animals that chow down most of it.

127
00:07:08,560 --> 00:07:10,800
More than 70% of the annual corn crop

128
00:07:10,900 --> 00:07:12,600
will go to feed livestock.

129
00:07:12,700 --> 00:07:16,680
But field corn and sweet corn aren't the only kernels

130
00:07:16,780 --> 00:07:18,080
on the cob.

131
00:07:18,180 --> 00:07:20,520
Perhaps the corn with the most fun appeal

132
00:07:20,620 --> 00:07:23,820
is a little delicacy called popcorn.

133
00:07:23,920 --> 00:07:27,460
We believe that the oldest kind of corn is popcorn,

134
00:07:27,560 --> 00:07:30,620
and that has a hard starch, and it's

135
00:07:30,621 --> 00:07:34,820
It also has to have an intact covering around the outside of it

136
00:07:34,920 --> 00:07:38,260
in order for it to actually be able to build up steam

137
00:07:38,360 --> 00:07:45,560
inside the corn and have the corn pop to make a flake.

138
00:07:45,660 --> 00:07:50,580
Popcorn stores water in tiny pockets of soft starch.

139
00:07:50,680 --> 00:07:53,780
As the kernel is heated, the water turns to steam,

140
00:07:53,880 --> 00:07:56,080
and the hard outer surface withstands the pressure

141
00:07:56,180 --> 00:07:57,620
as long as possible.

142
00:07:57,621 --> 00:08:00,960
Then, pop goes the corn.

143
00:08:04,260 --> 00:08:07,560
Popcorn comprises less than 2% of the United States corn crop

144
00:08:07,660 --> 00:08:10,660
and is grown in 25 states.

145
00:08:10,760 --> 00:08:14,740
But almost all US corn is grown in the Corn Belt, which

146
00:08:14,840 --> 00:08:18,480
consists of 12 states in the Midwest.

147
00:08:18,580 --> 00:08:24,420
Indiana, Illinois, Ohio, and Iowa produce 50% of America's

148
00:08:24,520 --> 00:08:26,820
total corn supply.

149
00:08:26,821 --> 00:08:28,820
Iowa is number one.

150
00:08:28,920 --> 00:08:31,560
It's very well adapted to the United States,

151
00:08:31,660 --> 00:08:35,060
especially the corn belt here in the Midwest.

152
00:08:35,160 --> 00:08:37,960
It's really a tropical crop, warm season crop.

153
00:08:38,060 --> 00:08:40,900
But the growing season here, the precipitation,

154
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:44,440
the rainfall that we get, the amount of light that we get,

155
00:08:44,540 --> 00:08:48,640
all those things combine to just be a wonderful, perfect

156
00:08:48,740 --> 00:08:51,220
environment for it to grow in.

157
00:08:51,320 --> 00:08:53,340
But no matter where corn grows,

158
00:08:53,440 --> 00:08:56,340
adaptation and versatility have made it for some

159
00:08:56,341 --> 00:08:59,140
the 21st century's gold.

160
00:08:59,240 --> 00:09:03,740
That's partly because it's not just for food.

161
00:09:03,840 --> 00:09:06,980
You'll find corn in over 400 non-food items,

162
00:09:07,080 --> 00:09:10,480
from adhesives to explosives.

163
00:09:10,580 --> 00:09:13,100
There are a huge number of uses.

164
00:09:13,200 --> 00:09:15,000
And what a lot of people don't understand

165
00:09:15,100 --> 00:09:18,100
is in terms of starch, there's more starch

166
00:09:18,200 --> 00:09:22,200
used in consumer goods, what we call bio-based products,

167
00:09:22,300 --> 00:09:23,740
than there is as food.

168
00:09:23,741 --> 00:09:28,740
of the starch, sold as starch, ends up in things like charcoal

169
00:09:28,840 --> 00:09:33,740
briquettes, adhesives, paper, plastics.

170
00:09:33,840 --> 00:09:35,480
Plastics?

171
00:09:35,580 --> 00:09:38,680
We think of plastic as a petroleum-based product.

172
00:09:38,780 --> 00:09:41,900
But corn will do the job too.

173
00:09:42,000 --> 00:09:45,700
NatureWorks in Blair, Nebraska, is a leader in the technology

174
00:09:45,800 --> 00:09:50,700
of transforming plant sugars into performance plastics.

175
00:09:50,800 --> 00:09:53,700
It's the first company to produce plastic from a plant

176
00:09:53,740 --> 00:09:58,540
from a 100% annually renewable resource.

177
00:09:58,541 --> 00:10:03,680
It's a new biopolymer called polylactic acid, or PLA,

178
00:10:03,681 --> 00:10:06,560
that shares its name with a company that makes it,

179
00:10:06,561 --> 00:10:08,220
NatureWorks.

180
00:10:08,221 --> 00:10:10,620
We can produce a crop of corn in about 100 days,

181
00:10:10,621 --> 00:10:12,460
not 100 million years.

182
00:10:12,461 --> 00:10:14,860
The production process from planting a seed of corn

183
00:10:14,861 --> 00:10:18,160
to making PLA has a much smaller environmental footprint

184
00:10:18,161 --> 00:10:20,300
than oil-based plastics.

185
00:10:20,301 --> 00:10:22,760
We have reduced the consumption of fossil fuels

186
00:10:22,761 --> 00:10:26,360
and we produce 80% to 90% less greenhouse gases

187
00:10:26,460 --> 00:10:29,400
than oil-based processes.

188
00:10:29,500 --> 00:10:32,800
Field corn is put through a milling process

189
00:10:32,900 --> 00:10:36,900
that separates the starch, oil, and protein.

190
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:38,740
Enzymes added to the starch convert it

191
00:10:38,840 --> 00:10:43,020
to a type of sugar called dextrose.

192
00:10:43,120 --> 00:10:46,480
The dextrose is then fermented with yeast

193
00:10:46,580 --> 00:10:49,420
to produce lactic acid.

194
00:10:49,421 --> 00:10:52,660
A proprietary process removes excess water

195
00:10:52,760 --> 00:10:55,560
and crystallizes the lactic acid into the Nature Works

196
00:10:55,660 --> 00:10:56,460
biopolymer.

197
00:10:59,760 --> 00:11:02,460
Within a matter of days of a corn kernel being received

198
00:11:02,560 --> 00:11:05,160
on this site, then the corn milling operation

199
00:11:05,260 --> 00:11:08,680
is converted into the Nature Works plastic.

200
00:11:08,780 --> 00:11:11,080
This bioplastic is shipped in resin form

201
00:11:11,180 --> 00:11:13,980
to customers around the globe.

202
00:11:14,080 --> 00:11:16,880
The resin is then converted into this family of products

203
00:11:16,980 --> 00:11:18,320
you see here on this table.

204
00:11:18,321 --> 00:11:23,020
Everything from plastic can be used to make a gift card

205
00:11:23,120 --> 00:11:28,860
to a clear food container to a foam-type product.

206
00:11:28,960 --> 00:11:30,960
We also have drink cups.

207
00:11:31,060 --> 00:11:34,840
The fiber made from PLA is real soft and easily printable.

208
00:11:34,940 --> 00:11:37,280
You see some shirts and other types of garments

209
00:11:37,380 --> 00:11:38,780
here behind me.

210
00:11:38,880 --> 00:11:43,420
It's easily identified by the NatureWorks logo.

211
00:11:43,520 --> 00:11:46,240
And this bioplastic costs about the same to produce

212
00:11:46,241 --> 00:11:49,540
as petroleum-based plastics, while decomposing

213
00:11:49,640 --> 00:11:52,780
in as little as 40 to 80 days when composted properly.

214
00:11:55,980 --> 00:11:58,980
For companies like Wilkinson Industries,

215
00:11:59,080 --> 00:12:05,000
making plastic food containers starts when the Nature Works

216
00:12:05,100 --> 00:12:11,200
bioplastic is sent through an extruder, where it melts into a

217
00:12:11,300 --> 00:12:12,800
sheet of clear plastic.

218
00:12:12,801 --> 00:12:16,800
Cooled and measured for thickness,

219
00:12:16,900 --> 00:12:19,800
it's wound onto rolls and sent to a heating and shaping

220
00:12:19,900 --> 00:12:22,740
machine called the thermoformer.

221
00:12:22,840 --> 00:12:25,340
The glass of the material exits the oven,

222
00:12:25,440 --> 00:12:29,180
goes into the mold assembly, where the mold comes in contact

223
00:12:29,280 --> 00:12:30,280
with the material.

224
00:12:30,380 --> 00:12:34,160
It closes from the top and from the bottom.

225
00:12:34,260 --> 00:12:36,260
An air pressure and vacuum system

226
00:12:36,360 --> 00:12:38,500
force the plastic into the mold.

227
00:12:38,600 --> 00:12:42,800
And seconds later, the plastic piece is complete.

228
00:12:42,801 --> 00:12:45,500
The finished containers are boxed and shipped,

229
00:12:45,600 --> 00:12:48,300
ready to appear on a deli or bakery shelf near you.

230
00:12:51,040 --> 00:12:54,040
Corn may be the holy grail for plastic,

231
00:12:54,140 --> 00:12:58,340
but exploiting its full potential is still on the horizon.

232
00:12:58,440 --> 00:13:02,860
Under high heat, NatureWorks performance is compromised.

233
00:13:02,960 --> 00:13:05,660
So for the time being, you won't find it on a car bumper

234
00:13:05,760 --> 00:13:07,600
or on the space shuttle.

235
00:13:07,700 --> 00:13:09,460
What you're looking at here is really

236
00:13:09,560 --> 00:13:11,760
the first generation of NatureWorks polymers.

237
00:13:11,761 --> 00:13:15,000
We continue to do research and continue to expand the breadth

238
00:13:15,100 --> 00:13:16,000
of application.

239
00:13:18,160 --> 00:13:20,800
21st century corn is a marvel of science

240
00:13:20,900 --> 00:13:22,900
and agriculture.

241
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:25,400
But it didn't start out that way.

242
00:13:25,500 --> 00:13:29,420
From the grasslands of Mexico to the mapping of its genome,

243
00:13:29,520 --> 00:13:32,420
corn has undergone an amazing transformation.

244
00:13:32,520 --> 00:13:36,320
And to create super corn is the next leap for both man

245
00:13:36,420 --> 00:13:37,420
and kernel.

246
00:13:41,760 --> 00:13:45,360
For 180 days, these wild-looking green stalks

247
00:13:45,460 --> 00:13:49,700
will flourish under the North American sky.

248
00:13:49,800 --> 00:13:54,640
But the irony is that corn is anything but wild.

249
00:13:54,740 --> 00:13:57,900
Corn has been domesticated, which means it can't grow

250
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:00,420
without human intervention.

251
00:14:00,520 --> 00:14:04,120
And not just some corn, but all of it, every kernel.

252
00:14:04,220 --> 00:14:06,460
There are no natural populations of corn

253
00:14:06,560 --> 00:14:08,820
like we normally see in an Iowa cornfield.

254
00:14:08,920 --> 00:14:09,820
It doesn't exist naturally.

255
00:14:09,920 --> 00:14:11,760
Corn is entirely different.

256
00:14:11,761 --> 00:14:15,500
a man-made phenomenon.

257
00:14:15,600 --> 00:14:18,400
If we weren't here to plant, water, fertilize,

258
00:14:18,500 --> 00:14:21,540
and harvest it, corn would disappear from the planet

259
00:14:21,640 --> 00:14:24,640
within a few years.

260
00:14:24,740 --> 00:14:28,580
The hypothetical scenario would go something like this.

261
00:14:28,680 --> 00:14:31,440
An ear of corn falls to the ground.

262
00:14:31,540 --> 00:14:35,080
Each seed or kernel struggles to germinate.

263
00:14:35,180 --> 00:14:39,860
The tightly packed seeds rob each other of vital nutrients.

264
00:14:39,861 --> 00:14:42,500
Such brutal competition chokes most, if not all,

265
00:14:42,600 --> 00:14:45,900
successful reproduction.

266
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:48,500
But corn will continue to thrive as long as farmers

267
00:14:48,600 --> 00:14:50,800
nurture it.

268
00:14:50,900 --> 00:14:55,100
And companies like Pioneer Hybrid are on the job.

269
00:14:55,200 --> 00:14:57,940
For almost a century, Pioneer has used science

270
00:14:58,040 --> 00:15:00,920
to create better corn.

271
00:15:01,020 --> 00:15:03,820
As one of the largest seed producers in the world,

272
00:15:03,920 --> 00:15:08,280
they ship corn seed to every continent except Antarctica.

273
00:15:08,281 --> 00:15:10,920
This is a seed crop and not a grain crop,

274
00:15:11,020 --> 00:15:14,420
so we bring it in and carefully handle it

275
00:15:14,520 --> 00:15:16,720
to preserve the integrity of that crop

276
00:15:16,820 --> 00:15:19,820
so that when the farmer plants it the following spring,

277
00:15:19,920 --> 00:15:22,360
it meets all expectations that both we and our farmer

278
00:15:22,460 --> 00:15:24,120
customers have.

279
00:15:24,220 --> 00:15:27,140
Corn is really an incredible crop.

280
00:15:27,240 --> 00:15:30,140
It's really dynamic in terms of what we can do with it

281
00:15:30,240 --> 00:15:34,080
to improve its characteristics.

282
00:15:34,180 --> 00:15:35,680
One way Pioneer works with corn

283
00:15:35,681 --> 00:15:38,680
breeding and the use of hybrid corn.

284
00:15:38,780 --> 00:15:42,580
When we talk about hybrid corn, which is really all the corn

285
00:15:42,680 --> 00:15:46,120
that we're using in modern society today, it's all hybrid.

286
00:15:46,220 --> 00:15:50,720
So it starts with two individual parents that are specifically

287
00:15:50,820 --> 00:15:56,240
mated to create great offspring, and those are hybrids.

288
00:15:56,340 --> 00:15:59,780
Hybrids are the combination of two distinct strains of corn,

289
00:15:59,880 --> 00:16:02,140
each with its own unique characteristics.

290
00:16:02,141 --> 00:16:05,040
characteristics.

291
00:16:05,140 --> 00:16:08,740
One might have a very large ear, and the other may have a very

292
00:16:08,840 --> 00:16:11,180
tall stalk.

293
00:16:11,280 --> 00:16:15,160
Individually, these strains are known as inbreds.

294
00:16:15,260 --> 00:16:19,920
Crossing these two inbreds together creates a hybrid.

295
00:16:20,020 --> 00:16:23,300
And hybrids often exhibit heterosis, which means they are

296
00:16:23,400 --> 00:16:24,500
better than their parents.

297
00:16:28,700 --> 00:16:31,340
This complex biological phenomenon of heterosis

298
00:16:31,341 --> 00:16:34,540
of today's corn industry.

299
00:16:34,640 --> 00:16:36,420
The wonderful thing for the seed companies

300
00:16:36,520 --> 00:16:38,540
is that if you collect the seed from a hybrid

301
00:16:38,640 --> 00:16:41,720
and plant it again, it doesn't have the same degree

302
00:16:41,820 --> 00:16:44,880
of superior performance as the hybrid itself.

303
00:16:44,980 --> 00:16:47,720
So growers have to go back to the seed company

304
00:16:47,820 --> 00:16:49,920
to buy hybrid seed again.

305
00:16:50,020 --> 00:16:52,500
That's actually a good thing, because seed companies

306
00:16:52,600 --> 00:16:56,000
have an incentive to invest in research and development.

307
00:16:56,100 --> 00:16:57,940
What modern day plant breeders did

308
00:16:58,040 --> 00:16:59,940
was they improved the plant's ability

309
00:16:59,941 --> 00:17:03,540
to withstand water stress, nitrogen deficiency,

310
00:17:03,640 --> 00:17:07,940
nutrient deficiency, temperature stress, insect attack,

311
00:17:08,040 --> 00:17:10,580
or disease attack.

312
00:17:10,680 --> 00:17:13,120
It may all look like modern science,

313
00:17:13,220 --> 00:17:16,180
but the breeding of corn really started thousands of years ago

314
00:17:16,280 --> 00:17:19,460
in central Mexico.

315
00:17:19,560 --> 00:17:23,060
But back then, corn looked a lot different.

316
00:17:23,160 --> 00:17:26,960
In fact, it didn't exist.

317
00:17:26,961 --> 00:17:31,800
It began as a wild grass called Teosinti, which only has a few

318
00:17:31,900 --> 00:17:34,440
small kernels and no real cob.

319
00:17:34,540 --> 00:17:36,440
It's an extraordinary feat, the evolution,

320
00:17:36,540 --> 00:17:38,940
the domestication of a crop.

321
00:17:39,040 --> 00:17:43,040
I'm not sure if we decided to domesticate corn now,

322
00:17:43,140 --> 00:17:46,620
whether we could actually muster the will as a society

323
00:17:46,720 --> 00:17:47,840
to do this.

324
00:17:47,940 --> 00:17:52,480
It's one of the real gifts of our ancestors to us.

325
00:17:52,580 --> 00:17:54,660
In fact, scientists continue to study

326
00:17:54,661 --> 00:17:58,560
to study the Teosinte plant to unlock its evolutionary secrets.

327
00:17:58,660 --> 00:18:00,000
They had discovered that there's

328
00:18:00,100 --> 00:18:02,900
just a handful of genes responsible for the major

329
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:06,400
morphological changes between Teosinte and corn.

330
00:18:06,500 --> 00:18:09,380
For example, the big one was the separation of the tassel

331
00:18:09,480 --> 00:18:11,740
from the ear.

332
00:18:11,840 --> 00:18:15,480
They probably saw mutations out in Teosinte fields that had

333
00:18:15,580 --> 00:18:17,920
the ear and tassel separate, and they selected for those.

334
00:18:18,020 --> 00:18:20,280
They may have noticed that they yielded more

335
00:18:20,380 --> 00:18:24,660
or had some other characteristic they were looking for.

336
00:18:24,661 --> 00:18:27,500
The tassel, which produces the pollen,

337
00:18:27,600 --> 00:18:30,560
is separated from the ear, which contains the egg cells that

338
00:18:30,660 --> 00:18:32,560
develop into kernels after pollination.

339
00:18:35,540 --> 00:18:38,540
The corn plant could and still can pollinate itself, as well

340
00:18:38,640 --> 00:18:39,780
as surrounding corn plants.

341
00:18:43,440 --> 00:18:46,220
The Native Americans achieved the Herculean task of turning

342
00:18:46,320 --> 00:18:47,420
Teosinti into corn.

343
00:18:49,980 --> 00:18:52,260
And they did it without a powerful and controversial

344
00:18:52,261 --> 00:18:57,260
scientific tool, genetic modification.

345
00:18:57,360 --> 00:19:00,260
If you talk about genetically modified corn,

346
00:19:00,360 --> 00:19:02,700
they would also be hybrids, but they're

347
00:19:02,800 --> 00:19:05,740
a special classification of a regulated product

348
00:19:05,840 --> 00:19:09,340
because we've brought in one single or maybe a few genes

349
00:19:09,440 --> 00:19:13,020
from another species to help us do something very specific

350
00:19:13,120 --> 00:19:15,120
in the plant.

351
00:19:15,220 --> 00:19:19,020
The revolution started in 1996 with what has become

352
00:19:19,021 --> 00:19:21,960
has become known as BT corn.

353
00:19:22,020 --> 00:19:26,120
The BT stands for Bacillus thuringiensis,

354
00:19:26,200 --> 00:19:29,700
a naturally occurring bacteria.

355
00:19:29,760 --> 00:19:32,760
This bacterium contains a protein toxic to one

356
00:19:32,840 --> 00:19:37,740
of corn's biggest enemies, the European corn borer.

357
00:19:37,800 --> 00:19:40,180
We express that protein in corn in a way

358
00:19:40,240 --> 00:19:43,780
that when the insect pest actually fed on the corn,

359
00:19:43,840 --> 00:19:48,280
it died as a result of taking that first bite.

360
00:19:48,281 --> 00:19:50,920
The corn borer has the ability to destroy millions

361
00:19:51,020 --> 00:19:53,220
of acres of corn.

362
00:19:53,320 --> 00:19:57,180
But because of the Bt trait, the economic damage this pest can

363
00:19:57,280 --> 00:20:01,020
cause has nearly been eliminated.

364
00:20:01,120 --> 00:20:05,200
Genetic modification is an exacting laboratory science

365
00:20:05,300 --> 00:20:08,000
in which DNA from one plant or animal

366
00:20:08,100 --> 00:20:10,440
is transferred into the embryo of another.

367
00:20:13,140 --> 00:20:17,100
Pioneer Hybrid has over 300 corn hybrids available for farmers

368
00:20:17,101 --> 00:20:19,540
in North America.

369
00:20:19,640 --> 00:20:23,640
Many are insect resistant, defying bugs

370
00:20:23,740 --> 00:20:28,840
like the Western bean cutworm that attacks the ear

371
00:20:28,940 --> 00:20:31,580
and the Western corn rootworm beetle larvae that

372
00:20:31,680 --> 00:20:35,020
attack the roots.

373
00:20:35,120 --> 00:20:38,660
In Pioneer's so-called fast corn greenhouse,

374
00:20:38,760 --> 00:20:41,460
the company has accelerated their evaluation of genetically

375
00:20:41,560 --> 00:20:43,860
engineered corn.

376
00:20:43,960 --> 00:20:46,600
In one month, we'd actually test about 1,000

377
00:20:46,601 --> 00:20:50,700
corn plants carrying about 100 different candidates

378
00:20:50,800 --> 00:20:51,940
to be traits.

379
00:20:52,040 --> 00:20:55,380
When those get harvested, new ones show up the next week

380
00:20:55,480 --> 00:20:58,980
with new traits to be tested.

381
00:20:59,080 --> 00:21:02,380
While future corn will certainly start in the lab,

382
00:21:02,480 --> 00:21:05,880
the genetically modified seed will have to be grown in test

383
00:21:05,980 --> 00:21:06,680
plots.

384
00:21:09,420 --> 00:21:11,400
Its cultivation near commercial cornfields

385
00:21:11,500 --> 00:21:16,600
is heavily restricted, since wind carries the corn's pollen.

386
00:21:16,601 --> 00:21:19,300
If untested, genetically altered crops

387
00:21:19,400 --> 00:21:22,740
could cross-pollinate with corn bound for the food supply.

388
00:21:22,840 --> 00:21:25,580
The result could prove economically disastrous

389
00:21:25,680 --> 00:21:28,380
and potentially harmful.

390
00:21:28,480 --> 00:21:31,580
Scenarios might include massive food recalls,

391
00:21:31,680 --> 00:21:34,920
unknown medical reactions in the general public,

392
00:21:35,020 --> 00:21:37,560
and crop loss.

393
00:21:37,660 --> 00:21:40,320
In the case of plant-derived pharmaceuticals,

394
00:21:40,420 --> 00:21:42,700
standard greenhouses aren't secure enough

395
00:21:42,800 --> 00:21:46,600
to grow certain seeds.

396
00:21:46,601 --> 00:21:50,040
So what about growing corn where the sun doesn't shine

397
00:21:50,140 --> 00:21:53,080
and the temperature always stays the same?

398
00:21:53,180 --> 00:21:55,780
We are driving into the Marengo Warehouse

399
00:21:55,880 --> 00:21:59,540
and Distribution Center deep in the hills of southern Indiana.

400
00:21:59,640 --> 00:22:02,520
We're literally driving into the side of a cliff,

401
00:22:02,620 --> 00:22:06,860
and up above us is about 160 feet of rock.

402
00:22:06,960 --> 00:22:10,660
This was once an active limestone mine

403
00:22:10,760 --> 00:22:14,060
that has subsequently been converted into a working

404
00:22:14,160 --> 00:22:15,420
warehouse.

405
00:22:15,421 --> 00:22:18,820
In the limestone caves, the temperature never fluctuates

406
00:22:18,920 --> 00:22:20,520
more than a few degrees.

407
00:22:20,620 --> 00:22:25,960
Sunlight never enters here, nor do any insects or animals.

408
00:22:26,060 --> 00:22:29,060
In this unlikely setting, founder Doug Ozenbaugh

409
00:22:29,160 --> 00:22:31,100
has created a contained atmosphere

410
00:22:31,200 --> 00:22:35,540
to grow any kind of corn for any purpose.

411
00:22:35,640 --> 00:22:40,120
It's called Controlled Farming Ventures, a blank slate

412
00:22:40,220 --> 00:22:42,920
to create any environment.

413
00:22:42,921 --> 00:22:46,760
And it all begins in the 2,000-square-foot test facility.

414
00:22:46,860 --> 00:22:48,160
For instance, if somebody was trying

415
00:22:48,260 --> 00:22:51,400
to develop a more drought-resistant line of corn,

416
00:22:51,500 --> 00:22:54,800
and they came upon a valuable discovery on, say,

417
00:22:54,900 --> 00:22:58,700
January the 5th, with 12 inches of snow outside here in Indiana,

418
00:22:58,800 --> 00:23:02,880
we can give them a 95-degree day with 20% humidity,

419
00:23:02,980 --> 00:23:05,200
and we can do that every day for the next three months

420
00:23:05,300 --> 00:23:06,440
while they test their crop.

421
00:23:06,540 --> 00:23:08,080
And I can guarantee you that you're not

422
00:23:08,180 --> 00:23:11,380
going to get an emergency rain to screw up your experiment.

423
00:23:11,381 --> 00:23:14,280
Ozenbaugh's underground ideas started in response

424
00:23:14,380 --> 00:23:17,060
to pharmaceutical research.

425
00:23:17,160 --> 00:23:18,220
The goal?

426
00:23:18,320 --> 00:23:22,200
Grow plants with special proteins for the safer, faster,

427
00:23:22,300 --> 00:23:25,620
and cheaper manufacturing of life-saving drugs.

428
00:23:25,720 --> 00:23:30,140
We've been able to design a system and a facility

429
00:23:30,240 --> 00:23:32,560
for about the same price as a greenhouse

430
00:23:32,660 --> 00:23:37,480
that enables you to really control all of the important

431
00:23:37,580 --> 00:23:41,380
variables that affect plant growth.

432
00:23:41,381 --> 00:23:44,180
a growth chamber for the 21st century.

433
00:23:44,280 --> 00:23:47,920
Controlled farming ventures puts corn into an alien world

434
00:23:48,020 --> 00:23:51,320
and makes it thrive.

435
00:23:51,420 --> 00:23:53,920
But back above ground, what happens

436
00:23:54,020 --> 00:23:56,620
to all that corn that grows every summer?

437
00:23:56,720 --> 00:24:00,440
How does it get into our cereal, muffins, and donuts?

438
00:24:04,880 --> 00:24:09,440
American farmers produce more than 364 million tons of corn

439
00:24:09,441 --> 00:24:15,440
That's enough to carpet 130,000 square miles with kernels.

440
00:24:19,440 --> 00:24:25,440
We're farming here and been in the business 47 years.

441
00:24:25,441 --> 00:24:28,440
We start planting our corn in April.

442
00:24:28,441 --> 00:24:31,440
We always hope to be done by May 10.

443
00:24:31,441 --> 00:24:35,440
That's a target date that we've set for ourselves,

444
00:24:35,441 --> 00:24:38,440
hoping that our yields will be better.

445
00:24:38,441 --> 00:24:42,040
in the fall harvest.

446
00:24:42,140 --> 00:24:43,680
This year, the Winger's family farm

447
00:24:43,780 --> 00:24:47,180
in central Wisconsin teams with 1,800 acres of corn.

448
00:24:49,680 --> 00:24:52,680
Not a kernel of it is the sweet corn destined to end up

449
00:24:52,780 --> 00:24:55,200
on your dinner table.

450
00:24:55,300 --> 00:24:58,000
It's dent corn, part of the 80 million acres that

451
00:24:58,100 --> 00:25:01,100
will be processed into everything from cereal to rat

452
00:25:01,200 --> 00:25:02,800
poison.

453
00:25:02,900 --> 00:25:04,300
That is, if it doesn't explode.

454
00:25:04,301 --> 00:25:08,140
explode.

455
00:25:08,240 --> 00:25:10,900
During the September harvest, they store some right in their

456
00:25:11,000 --> 00:25:12,340
own backyard.

457
00:25:12,440 --> 00:25:14,140
In those three grain bands behind me,

458
00:25:14,240 --> 00:25:17,040
we can store around 250,000 bushel.

459
00:25:17,140 --> 00:25:20,380
There's not enough corn storage at a commercial elevator to store

460
00:25:20,480 --> 00:25:23,260
it all for a whole year's supply.

461
00:25:23,360 --> 00:25:25,560
So you need to store your own corn.

462
00:25:25,660 --> 00:25:28,160
And then at times of the year when the market is better,

463
00:25:28,260 --> 00:25:31,960
you can take advantage of that.

464
00:25:32,060 --> 00:25:34,000
When the wingers are ready to sell,

465
00:25:34,001 --> 00:25:38,000
One buyer is just down the road.

466
00:25:38,100 --> 00:25:41,100
Didion Milling has been grinding corn for 35 years.

467
00:25:44,140 --> 00:25:45,940
Each truckload Kelly Wingers delivers

468
00:25:46,040 --> 00:25:47,640
holds four acres worth of kernels.

469
00:25:51,060 --> 00:25:54,160
And its first destination is a giant dryer.

470
00:25:54,260 --> 00:25:56,660
That dryer has a series of burners and high pressure

471
00:25:56,760 --> 00:25:57,960
fans in the bottom.

472
00:25:58,060 --> 00:26:01,600
So the corn is actually in a layer between what we call

473
00:26:01,601 --> 00:26:03,260
skins, two skins.

474
00:26:03,360 --> 00:26:06,880
And the corn gets fed into the top and travels toward the

475
00:26:06,980 --> 00:26:10,500
bottom, while hot air gets pushed up and out through the

476
00:26:10,600 --> 00:26:11,780
corn.

477
00:26:11,880 --> 00:26:14,220
We run the burners at about 180 degrees.

478
00:26:17,380 --> 00:26:19,980
Keeping the corn dry not only keeps it from spoiling

479
00:26:20,080 --> 00:26:23,860
too fast, but also from becoming a deadly hazard.

480
00:26:23,960 --> 00:26:26,920
If you have wet corn that's sitting in a silo very long,

481
00:26:27,020 --> 00:26:29,420
that's a perfect temperature to grow bacteria.

482
00:26:29,421 --> 00:26:33,420
So that bacteria, as it eats the corn, it creates heat.

483
00:26:33,520 --> 00:26:36,360
And that heat will eventually get to a critical mass

484
00:26:36,460 --> 00:26:40,660
where you can actually have a fire if you don't store the corn

485
00:26:40,760 --> 00:26:43,860
properly, if it's too wet.

486
00:26:43,960 --> 00:26:47,440
And with 21 silos that each hold the equivalent of 200

487
00:26:47,540 --> 00:26:51,180
truckloads of corn, keeping this powder keg of kernels cool

488
00:26:51,280 --> 00:26:51,980
is a challenge.

489
00:26:51,981 --> 00:26:59,220
Disasters like this 1998 grain elevator explosion in Wichita,

490
00:26:59,320 --> 00:27:04,020
Kansas, that killed two people, are a tragic example of the

491
00:27:04,120 --> 00:27:06,820
potential dangers.

492
00:27:06,920 --> 00:27:10,060
Corn can be kept for up to a year if the storage

493
00:27:10,160 --> 00:27:11,460
conditions are proper.

494
00:27:11,560 --> 00:27:14,800
So all these silos are equipped with aeration fans where we

495
00:27:14,900 --> 00:27:18,280
push cubic feet of air up through the corn, and that

496
00:27:18,281 --> 00:27:23,180
And that keeps the corn from having temperature spikes.

497
00:27:23,280 --> 00:27:26,180
These concrete monsters are as tall as a 12-story building.

498
00:27:29,060 --> 00:27:34,200
It takes two square miles of corn to fill just one silo.

499
00:27:34,300 --> 00:27:38,240
Corn is sent down this gravity chute into the mill system.

500
00:27:38,340 --> 00:27:39,640
And the first step that goes through there

501
00:27:39,740 --> 00:27:41,840
is the corn cleaning.

502
00:27:41,940 --> 00:27:46,900
Once cleaned, the kernels are cut into small pieces

503
00:27:46,901 --> 00:27:51,100
and sent through a complex system of sifters

504
00:27:51,200 --> 00:27:53,880
and air pressure chambers on their way

505
00:27:53,980 --> 00:27:54,880
to the grinding machines.

506
00:27:58,180 --> 00:28:00,540
In each grinding machine, two metal cylinders

507
00:28:00,640 --> 00:28:04,380
are positioned side by side and rotate toward each other

508
00:28:04,480 --> 00:28:05,280
at different speeds.

509
00:28:08,260 --> 00:28:11,400
Each cylinder has grooves, which will determine how fine

510
00:28:11,500 --> 00:28:13,100
the grind will be.

511
00:28:13,200 --> 00:28:16,200
The end products that come out of all this process

512
00:28:16,201 --> 00:28:19,100
which is the finest material that we ship.

513
00:28:19,200 --> 00:28:22,800
And which might end up in the batter of your buffalo wings,

514
00:28:22,900 --> 00:28:25,940
or even in your donut.

515
00:28:26,040 --> 00:28:29,720
They also produce cornmeal that could cover your English muffin

516
00:28:29,820 --> 00:28:34,420
or fill your corn chip, and even coarser cornmeal

517
00:28:34,520 --> 00:28:37,620
you can find on the bottom of a pizza.

518
00:28:37,720 --> 00:28:40,200
And when that coarse cornmeal is cooked,

519
00:28:40,300 --> 00:28:44,120
it'll puff up and get processed into your favorite cereals.

520
00:28:44,121 --> 00:28:47,020
materials.

521
00:28:47,120 --> 00:28:51,220
Even as this corn can be turned into food,

522
00:28:51,320 --> 00:28:55,140
its sticky characteristics make it a perfect binder

523
00:28:55,240 --> 00:28:59,140
to hold together such products as porcelain sinks

524
00:28:59,240 --> 00:29:01,040
and even rat poison pellets.

525
00:29:05,140 --> 00:29:07,640
Whether Didion's milled corn becomes a goodie you eat

526
00:29:07,740 --> 00:29:11,440
or a product you use, they ship it to clients around the world

527
00:29:11,540 --> 00:29:14,120
via truck and rail.

528
00:29:14,121 --> 00:29:16,960
We're actually inside a railroad car right now.

529
00:29:17,060 --> 00:29:19,060
So the bags that you see coming across

530
00:29:19,160 --> 00:29:21,760
are coming directly off the packaging lines.

531
00:29:21,860 --> 00:29:24,160
These bags each weigh about 55 pounds,

532
00:29:24,260 --> 00:29:27,160
and a bushel of corn weighs about 56 pounds.

533
00:29:27,260 --> 00:29:31,480
The railroad car will hold between 2,500 and 2,700

534
00:29:31,580 --> 00:29:34,980
of these bags, and that equates to an average tonnage

535
00:29:35,080 --> 00:29:39,580
of about 67 to 70 metric tons per railroad car.

536
00:29:39,680 --> 00:29:42,280
This loading is just the start of a global transportation

537
00:29:42,281 --> 00:29:48,220
phenomenon that literally helps feed the world.

538
00:29:48,320 --> 00:29:50,180
In collaboration with the United States Department

539
00:29:50,280 --> 00:29:53,720
of Agriculture, Didion Milling is one of the largest suppliers

540
00:29:53,820 --> 00:29:58,320
of US food aid to countries in need.

541
00:29:58,420 --> 00:30:02,440
They ship approximately 130,000 metric tons of a corn meal

542
00:30:02,540 --> 00:30:05,840
based nutrient rich food product that provides essential

543
00:30:05,940 --> 00:30:07,280
calories to sustain life.

544
00:30:07,281 --> 00:30:14,280
The same Midwestern corn nourishing the masses now also

545
00:30:14,380 --> 00:30:16,280
feeds our gas tanks.

546
00:30:16,380 --> 00:30:19,560
The ethanol craze is here, but is there enough corn

547
00:30:19,660 --> 00:30:22,500
to fuel ourselves and our cars?

548
00:30:27,800 --> 00:30:31,240
A near endless line of semi-trucks filled to the brim

549
00:30:31,340 --> 00:30:34,600
with a bounty of gold kernels.

550
00:30:34,601 --> 00:30:38,200
This is the entrance to VeriSun Energy's ethanol production

551
00:30:38,300 --> 00:30:44,540
plant in Charles City, Iowa, opened in 2007.

552
00:30:44,640 --> 00:30:48,640
By the end of the decade, more than 200 ethanol biorefineries

553
00:30:48,740 --> 00:30:53,860
will dot the landscape from Washington State to Georgia.

554
00:30:53,960 --> 00:30:58,260
Ethanol is a biofuel that reduces tailpipe carbon monoxide

555
00:30:58,360 --> 00:31:00,260
emissions by up to 30%.

556
00:31:00,360 --> 00:31:03,200
Ethanol is about ready to take over

557
00:31:03,201 --> 00:31:06,200
to take over as the number one user of corn in the United

558
00:31:06,300 --> 00:31:07,900
States.

559
00:31:08,000 --> 00:31:12,100
Converting corn to ethanol isn't that complicated.

560
00:31:12,200 --> 00:31:16,040
But to mass produce hundreds of thousands of gallons per day

561
00:31:16,140 --> 00:31:19,280
requires a detailed, coordinated, and streamlined

562
00:31:19,380 --> 00:31:20,980
system.

563
00:31:21,080 --> 00:31:23,080
Where we're at now is the starting point where

564
00:31:23,180 --> 00:31:24,080
everything begins.

565
00:31:24,180 --> 00:31:26,360
The corn comes in through here.

566
00:31:26,460 --> 00:31:29,820
A truck will weigh about 80,000 when it's full, which

567
00:31:29,920 --> 00:31:33,200
is about 30,000 for the truck, and about 50,000

568
00:31:33,201 --> 00:31:35,940
pounds of corn.

569
00:31:36,040 --> 00:31:38,780
If you're doing the math, that adds up to 45 million

570
00:31:38,880 --> 00:31:41,680
pounds of corn a week.

571
00:31:41,780 --> 00:31:44,040
Speed through here is very important.

572
00:31:44,140 --> 00:31:46,920
Typically, when the time a truck pulls onto the scale,

573
00:31:47,020 --> 00:31:49,880
we sample it, weigh it, and a truck pulls off,

574
00:31:49,980 --> 00:31:52,220
it's less than a minute.

575
00:31:52,320 --> 00:31:54,920
Once the corn is OK, the truck rolls over

576
00:31:55,020 --> 00:31:59,700
to the offloading area, where it dumps its precious cargo

577
00:31:59,800 --> 00:32:02,700
into a pit.

578
00:32:02,701 --> 00:32:07,400
From the pit, the corn travels up a grain land,

579
00:32:07,500 --> 00:32:11,200
where it flows into the top of the silos.

580
00:32:11,300 --> 00:32:13,140
We have four silos together.

581
00:32:13,240 --> 00:32:16,040
They hold about 2.2 million bushels of corn.

582
00:32:16,140 --> 00:32:20,860
We have a small fifth silo here where we actually screen the corn,

583
00:32:20,960 --> 00:32:22,760
get any impurities out, and we actually

584
00:32:22,860 --> 00:32:25,760
grind the corn into a corn flour.

585
00:32:25,860 --> 00:32:27,960
Behind me is our hammer mills.

586
00:32:28,060 --> 00:32:29,700
That's where we grind our corn.

587
00:32:29,800 --> 00:32:32,000
We have a dry mill process.

588
00:32:32,001 --> 00:32:34,500
Once the corn is grounded to a flour,

589
00:32:34,600 --> 00:32:39,500
we send it over a conveyor where it enters our process.

590
00:32:39,600 --> 00:32:44,740
We grind about 120,000 bushels of corn a day.

591
00:32:44,840 --> 00:32:49,860
Conveyors move the corn flour into the cook area,

592
00:32:49,960 --> 00:32:52,580
where it's soaked with water and enzymes, which

593
00:32:52,680 --> 00:32:56,560
break down the corn's starch into sugar.

594
00:32:56,660 --> 00:32:58,560
For hundreds of years, this basic process

595
00:32:58,660 --> 00:33:01,260
has also been used to ferment alcohol

596
00:33:01,261 --> 00:33:05,100
and make liquor, like corn whiskey.

597
00:33:05,200 --> 00:33:09,440
Now called mash, it cooks at 225 degrees Fahrenheit

598
00:33:09,540 --> 00:33:12,640
to kill any bacteria.

599
00:33:12,740 --> 00:33:14,840
The corn mash then cools and travels

600
00:33:14,940 --> 00:33:19,880
to an 800,000 gallon fermentation tank, one of seven

601
00:33:19,980 --> 00:33:22,440
where yeast is added.

602
00:33:22,540 --> 00:33:24,920
You can't make alcohol without yeast.

603
00:33:25,020 --> 00:33:27,620
The yeast eat the sugar, and one of their byproducts

604
00:33:27,720 --> 00:33:30,920
is the ethanol.

605
00:33:30,921 --> 00:33:34,820
After 48 hours of fermenting, it's termed beer,

606
00:33:34,920 --> 00:33:37,220
although you wouldn't want to drink it.

607
00:33:37,320 --> 00:33:40,760
After fermentation, one of the most important processes

608
00:33:40,860 --> 00:33:44,300
in ethanol production is actually purifying the ethanol.

609
00:33:44,400 --> 00:33:46,200
We do that behind me.

610
00:33:46,300 --> 00:33:49,240
We have three distillation columns.

611
00:33:49,340 --> 00:33:51,480
The first one is what we call a beer column.

612
00:33:51,580 --> 00:33:55,340
We actually separate the alcohol from the solids.

613
00:33:55,440 --> 00:33:58,440
The mash runs through a series of metal plates,

614
00:33:58,441 --> 00:34:03,220
And under high heat emits a water and alcohol vapor

615
00:34:03,320 --> 00:34:06,440
that rises and moves through two additional distillation

616
00:34:06,540 --> 00:34:09,920
columns to purify the alcohol to 190 proof.

617
00:34:13,780 --> 00:34:17,360
The final step sends the vapor to a molecular sieve.

618
00:34:17,460 --> 00:34:20,300
There, the water and alcohol vapors are separated

619
00:34:20,400 --> 00:34:22,600
as the water is captured and absorbed by millions

620
00:34:22,700 --> 00:34:25,700
of small clay pellets.

621
00:34:25,701 --> 00:34:31,200
What's left is 200-proof pure alcohol.

622
00:34:31,300 --> 00:34:36,540
Contrary to what you may think, ethanol isn't anything new.

623
00:34:36,640 --> 00:34:39,920
Ironically, it was ethanol that fueled Henry Ford's early Model

624
00:34:40,020 --> 00:34:44,660
T, the first flexible fuel vehicle.

625
00:34:44,760 --> 00:34:48,220
And Ford might be surprised that after 100 years, ethanol

626
00:34:48,320 --> 00:34:51,400
is in the alternative fuel category.

627
00:34:51,500 --> 00:34:55,700
Critics question, is ethanol really environmentally friendly?

628
00:34:55,701 --> 00:34:59,400
To grow the corn and convert it to ethanol requires nitrogen

629
00:34:59,500 --> 00:35:04,340
for fertilizer and diesel equipment on the farms,

630
00:35:04,440 --> 00:35:08,740
as well as natural gas at the processing plant.

631
00:35:08,840 --> 00:35:12,420
Some experts estimate that it takes one gallon of fossil fuel

632
00:35:12,520 --> 00:35:14,960
to produce 1.3 gallons of ethanol.

633
00:35:18,120 --> 00:35:22,020
Still, there are already nearly 6 million flexible fuel vehicles

634
00:35:22,120 --> 00:35:25,700
traveling America's roads.

635
00:35:25,701 --> 00:35:28,740
They're called flexible because these cars are able to use

636
00:35:28,840 --> 00:35:33,240
either unleaded gasoline or the current ethanol standard, E85.

637
00:35:37,240 --> 00:35:41,240
E85 releases 80% less carcinogens, such as benzene,

638
00:35:41,340 --> 00:35:43,260
into the air than unleaded gasoline.

639
00:35:46,120 --> 00:35:50,760
In 2006, ethanol use in the US reduced CO2-equivalent

640
00:35:50,860 --> 00:35:55,700
greenhouse gas emissions by approximately 8 million tons.

641
00:35:55,701 --> 00:35:59,700
That's equal to removing the annual emissions of more than

642
00:35:59,800 --> 00:36:01,700
1.2 million cars.

643
00:36:04,700 --> 00:36:08,640
But is there enough corn to feed us and fuel our cars?

644
00:36:08,740 --> 00:36:10,740
We're not going to run out of corn.

645
00:36:10,840 --> 00:36:12,580
We have plenty of corn to go around,

646
00:36:12,680 --> 00:36:14,120
and if we start running out of corn,

647
00:36:14,220 --> 00:36:18,060
the price will go up and farmers will plant more acres to corn.

648
00:36:18,160 --> 00:36:20,760
Detractors, however, point out that this will mean less

649
00:36:20,860 --> 00:36:24,300
acreage available for other crops.

650
00:36:24,301 --> 00:36:27,640
Nevertheless, the corn ethanol story has an extremely useful

651
00:36:27,740 --> 00:36:29,300
co-product.

652
00:36:31,000 --> 00:36:34,240
Dried distillers grains are the portions of corn that can't be

653
00:36:34,340 --> 00:36:37,640
fermented, but they contain highly valued nutrients for

654
00:36:37,740 --> 00:36:38,780
livestock.

655
00:36:38,880 --> 00:36:42,780
This is where we actually process the distiller's grain and dry

656
00:36:42,880 --> 00:36:44,420
the distiller's grain out.

657
00:36:44,520 --> 00:36:48,160
The solids come out into a conveying system that takes it

658
00:36:48,260 --> 00:36:51,220
through two big rotary drum dryers, where we remove all

659
00:36:51,320 --> 00:36:52,660
but 10% of the water.

660
00:36:52,661 --> 00:36:56,660
So we have a 90% dry, solid product.

661
00:36:56,760 --> 00:36:57,960
You can see this.

662
00:36:58,060 --> 00:37:00,860
This is a mixture of protein, fiber, and oil.

663
00:37:00,960 --> 00:37:02,700
This is what the animals want.

664
00:37:02,800 --> 00:37:05,700
This has always been viewed probably prior to this industry

665
00:37:05,800 --> 00:37:07,100
as a waste product.

666
00:37:07,200 --> 00:37:09,240
But now it's just a viable product that we use

667
00:37:09,340 --> 00:37:12,120
in the feed industry.

668
00:37:12,220 --> 00:37:15,720
It takes a mere 65 hours to both process a kernel of corn

669
00:37:15,820 --> 00:37:21,060
into ethanol and ready it for shipping by rail.

670
00:37:21,061 --> 00:37:24,060
And it only took about 100 days for the corn to grow.

671
00:37:26,560 --> 00:37:29,560
Much of that corn is hybridized, specifically

672
00:37:29,660 --> 00:37:32,400
to get the ethanol to fossil fuel ratio higher.

673
00:37:34,600 --> 00:37:37,780
But in the future, it may be possible to convert even the corn

674
00:37:37,880 --> 00:37:41,620
stalks, leaves, and husks into ethanol fuel

675
00:37:41,720 --> 00:37:46,060
through the science of cellulosic technology.

676
00:37:46,160 --> 00:37:49,480
The US government is investing hundreds of millions

677
00:37:49,481 --> 00:37:52,980
of dollars in research to try to figure out that question.

678
00:37:53,080 --> 00:37:56,960
Will cellulosic feedstocks play an important role

679
00:37:57,060 --> 00:38:00,620
in fueling our transportation, our cars and buses?

680
00:38:00,720 --> 00:38:03,220
We don't know yet.

681
00:38:03,320 --> 00:38:06,600
For now, kernels in our gas tanks fuel our cars.

682
00:38:06,700 --> 00:38:09,200
But corn converts to another fuel that may not

683
00:38:09,300 --> 00:38:11,600
be so good for us.

684
00:38:11,700 --> 00:38:15,940
In soda, candy, and about 2,000 other foods,

685
00:38:15,941 --> 00:38:19,380
It's sugar made from corn, a.k.a.

686
00:38:19,480 --> 00:38:21,180
high fructose corn syrup.

687
00:38:25,280 --> 00:38:28,680
The soft drink industry generates almost $48 billion

688
00:38:28,780 --> 00:38:33,160
a year, and Americans consume more than 15 billion gallons

689
00:38:33,260 --> 00:38:34,420
of its drinks.

690
00:38:34,520 --> 00:38:37,360
But what does soda have to do with corn?

691
00:38:37,460 --> 00:38:40,300
It's all about the sugar.

692
00:38:40,400 --> 00:38:43,740
On average, corn-based sweeteners comprise more than 50%

693
00:38:43,741 --> 00:38:46,580
of the US sweetener market.

694
00:38:46,680 --> 00:38:50,320
And the most prevalent of all is that super sweet substance,

695
00:38:50,420 --> 00:38:52,820
high fructose corn syrup.

696
00:38:52,920 --> 00:38:55,860
Corn syrup can be found in an incredible array

697
00:38:55,960 --> 00:38:57,920
of your pantry's products.

698
00:38:58,020 --> 00:39:02,300
Everything from simple ketchup to mayonnaise

699
00:39:02,400 --> 00:39:06,900
to sports drinks to salad dressings.

700
00:39:07,000 --> 00:39:10,640
Not to mention ice cream, bread,

701
00:39:10,740 --> 00:39:13,740
and even baby formula.

702
00:39:13,741 --> 00:39:16,640
But there's a downside to the deliciousness.

703
00:39:16,740 --> 00:39:18,840
Some have targeted high fructose corn syrup

704
00:39:18,940 --> 00:39:21,840
as a prime catalyst fueling America's growing problem

705
00:39:21,940 --> 00:39:23,140
with obesity.

706
00:39:25,160 --> 00:39:27,460
High fructose corn syrup is manufactured

707
00:39:27,560 --> 00:39:34,120
from field corn through a process known as wet milling.

708
00:39:34,220 --> 00:39:37,100
The ultimate goal is to isolate the starch present in the

709
00:39:37,200 --> 00:39:41,140
kernels, since that's the stuff that will convert to sugar.

710
00:39:41,141 --> 00:39:45,620
The first step then in wet milling then is to take this corn

711
00:39:45,720 --> 00:39:48,620
and add it to an acidic solution of water.

712
00:39:48,720 --> 00:39:53,480
And this softens the grain so that when we go to mill it,

713
00:39:53,580 --> 00:39:57,660
what we want to do is keep that germ in as large a particle

714
00:39:57,760 --> 00:40:00,360
as possible.

715
00:40:00,460 --> 00:40:03,500
Like the yolk of an egg, the germ is the embryo

716
00:40:03,600 --> 00:40:07,240
and contains oil, while the starch resides elsewhere

717
00:40:07,340 --> 00:40:09,660
in the kernel.

718
00:40:09,661 --> 00:40:12,900
Here a specially equipped blender grinds the corn and water

719
00:40:13,000 --> 00:40:14,600
solution without damaging the germ.

720
00:40:17,600 --> 00:40:20,200
Once blended, the germ will float to the surface

721
00:40:20,300 --> 00:40:22,900
because of its high oil content.

722
00:40:23,000 --> 00:40:26,980
The isolated germ can then be squeezed to a pulp,

723
00:40:27,080 --> 00:40:30,020
and its corn oil will eventually wind up in your frying pan.

724
00:40:33,120 --> 00:40:37,560
The remaining portions of the kernel get finely ground.

725
00:40:37,561 --> 00:40:41,160
After filtering out the fiber, the liquid and starch separate

726
00:40:41,260 --> 00:40:44,660
in a settling trough.

727
00:40:44,760 --> 00:40:49,300
The result, 99.7% pure cornstarch,

728
00:40:49,400 --> 00:40:52,100
which is then processed on the molecular level

729
00:40:52,200 --> 00:40:56,520
into both fructose and glucose sugars.

730
00:40:56,620 --> 00:41:01,180
The combination of these sugars results in both corn syrup

731
00:41:01,280 --> 00:41:03,880
and high fructose corn syrup.

732
00:41:03,980 --> 00:41:05,880
The best way to think of it is a blend

733
00:41:05,881 --> 00:41:09,880
of different sugars combined with enzymes to make a liquid.

734
00:41:09,980 --> 00:41:11,620
It's plenty sweet.

735
00:41:11,720 --> 00:41:15,620
For example, you could eat this entire plate of apples.

736
00:41:15,720 --> 00:41:20,120
Six of these apples would equal the sweetening power

737
00:41:20,220 --> 00:41:23,100
of this little bit of high fructose corn syrup

738
00:41:23,200 --> 00:41:29,040
that is so nutritionally bereft of value.

739
00:41:29,140 --> 00:41:31,580
High fructose corn syrup has made its way

740
00:41:31,680 --> 00:41:34,140
into thousands of packaged food items.

741
00:41:34,141 --> 00:41:38,780
And some experts believe its chemical structure

742
00:41:38,880 --> 00:41:41,340
may trick the body in harmful ways.

743
00:41:41,440 --> 00:41:44,780
The theory is that high fructose corn syrup

744
00:41:44,880 --> 00:41:47,780
depresses the production of hormones

745
00:41:47,880 --> 00:41:49,280
that regulate our appetite.

746
00:41:49,380 --> 00:41:54,300
So we are actually less responsive biologically

747
00:41:54,400 --> 00:41:59,400
to the chemical messengers that tell our body to stop eating.

748
00:41:59,500 --> 00:42:04,140
And that's something regular table sugar doesn't do.

749
00:42:04,141 --> 00:42:06,340
But some argue there are other factors that contribute

750
00:42:06,440 --> 00:42:11,240
to America's weight, not just high fructose corn serum.

751
00:42:11,340 --> 00:42:13,980
It deals with calorie intake.

752
00:42:14,080 --> 00:42:15,880
It deals with exercise.

753
00:42:15,980 --> 00:42:19,380
It deals with a whole gamut of nutrition.

754
00:42:19,480 --> 00:42:22,500
So people who are blaming one particular product,

755
00:42:22,600 --> 00:42:26,600
I feel is erroneous and a wrong thing to do.

756
00:42:26,700 --> 00:42:29,240
The dilemma is that it's likely to remain

757
00:42:29,340 --> 00:42:34,140
a cheap and pervasive ingredient for years to come.

758
00:42:34,141 --> 00:42:38,620
Now the problem is that there are no commercially

759
00:42:38,621 --> 00:42:43,480
economical solutions to replace high fructose corn syrup.

760
00:42:44,520 --> 00:42:47,220
So with corn, it seems that every kernel

761
00:42:47,221 --> 00:42:51,500
is filled with promise, debate, and power.

762
00:42:52,300 --> 00:42:56,740
From super corn to biofuel to feeding the masses,

763
00:42:57,640 --> 00:43:00,620
what we choose to do with this golden opportunity

764
00:43:00,621 --> 00:43:04,120
might just take us to a field of dreams.

765
00:43:04,140 --> 00:43:07,580
In the meantime,

